# DESCRIPTION
# Find the sample proportion, margin of error and confidence interval for a
# sample survey of voters.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

# This problem is derived from a homework problem in Introductory Statistics,
# licensed by OpenStax under a Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY 4.0).
# Modified for WeBWorK by Michael Stassen   mstassen(at)fitchburgstate(dot)edu

## DBsubject('Introductory Statistics')
## DBchapter('7')
## DBsection('1')
## KEYWORDS('sample survey', 'sample proportion', 'margin of error', 'confidence interval')
## Author('Michael Stassen')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',        "PGML.pl",
    "contextPercent.pl",    "PGchoicemacros.pl",
    "PGnumericalmacros.pl", "PGstatisticsmacros.pl",
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("LimitedNumeric");
loadMacros("fixedPrecision.pl");    # must come after Context is set.

$conf = list_random(90, 95, 99);

$alpha = 1 - $conf / 100;

$x = random(310,  360);
$n = random(1100, 1300, 50);

Context("Percent");
Context()->flags->set(
    decimalPlaces => 1,
    tolerance     => .0005,
);
$p    = Percent(Round($x / $n, 3));
$pdec = Round($x / $n, 3);
$q    = 1 - $p;

$z = Round(normal_distr(0.5 - $alpha / 2), 4);

$E = Percent($z * sqrt($p * $q / $n));

$p1 = Percent($p - $E);
$p2 = Percent($p + $E);

BEGIN_PGML
According to a recent survey of [$n] people, [$x] feel that the governor 
of a particular state is doing an acceptable job. We wish to calculate a [$conf]% 
confidence interval for the percent of people who feel the governor is doing an
acceptable job. (Round percents to the nearest 0.1%)

a. What is the sample proportion?[____]{$p} 
b. What is the margin of error for a [$conf]% confidence interval?[____]{$E}  
e. The [$conf]% confidence interval is from [____]{$p1} to [____]{$p2}.  

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) The sample proportion is the number of "successes" (in this case, those that feel
the governor is doing an acceptable job) divided by the number in the survey.  In this case
[``` \hat{p} = \frac{[$x]}{[$n]} = [$p]. ```]

b) For a population proportion, the margin of error is 
[``` ME = z_{1-\alpha/2} \sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n}}```]
where [`z_{1-\alpha/2}`] is the [`z`]-score corresponding to the confidence interval given.  for a [$conf]% confidence interval this is [$z].
[```ME = ([$z]) \sqrt{ \frac{([$pdec])([$pdec*(-1)+1])}{[$n]}} = [$E]```]

c) The confidence interval then is 
[```(\hat{p} - ME, \hat{p}+ME) = ([$p1],[$p2])```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
